  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-506141</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following the recent escalation of hostilities in Gaza, many families from the northern areas and Gaza City are displaced to the southern areas of Gaza Valley. This mass movement has resulted in overcrowding in the southern areas, where families find themselves either in shelters or hosted by others. Unfortunately, a significant number of displaced individuals lack the financial means to meettheir basic needs, including food and water in addition to very limited availability of food and water.
In response to this pressing situation, ESDC is planning to distribute vegetable baskets. The target recipients include 3.000 families who have been displaced. Each basket will contain: tomato, cucumber, orange, lemon, pepper (sweet and chilly), eggplant, cauliflower, and zucchini with a total amount of 100 NIS per basket per family.
ESDC will collaborate with the Rafah Agricultural Cooperative under this intervention.Recognizing the importance of food supply and the need to support farmers as they are actively producing crops, the cooperative approached ESDC for assistance to market the vegetables of farmers. Farmer Samir Khdeir, a trusted member of the cooperative, will facilitate the collection of crops from fellow farmers and coordinate the delivery to ESDC employee Ahmed Abu Haloub who is displaced and stays at one of the schools that we will be targeting. Ahmed, along with 8 volunteers, will then pack the vegetables and deliver them to distribution points. ESDC staff and the volunteers will supervise thewhole process of the distribution. Each HHs representative of each family will sign a receipt for the food basket, providing their ID number and full name.
All proposed vegetable items by ESDC are available and currently being produced on the farms. However, it&apos;s worth noting that some items, such as zucchini and eggplant may need cooking but all the families that were asked about it confirmed their willingness to receive such items. families are able to cook using cartons and wood for a fire.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org ref="NL-KVK-27108436" role="4" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib has been active in the Occupied Palestinian Territory/Israel (OPT/I) since 1982 and working with Palestinian refugees in Lebanon since the mid 1980s. It has built up a strong network of relationships with local NGOs and counts among its localcounterparts some of the largest and most professional NGOs in various sectors. It has accompanied these NGOs in their development from ad hoc volunteer groups to sizeable professional institutions with national outreach.  At the same time the conceptualisation of the programme has evolved from three country programs in the early 1990s to a program for Palestinians in the Middle East (PME) working withPalestinians in OPT, Israel and Lebanon.

The programme has changed over the years with the establishment of the Palestinian Authority (PA) in the mid-1990s from a focus on support and service provision at a grassroots level and grassroots mobilization, to working towards influencing the establishment of the PA, its lawmaking and supporting building a democratic state # this involved supporting efforts of Palestinian civil society to build the basis for a democratic Palestinian state with room for civil participation, ensuring inclusion of vulnerable groups, refugees and women, and shaping its laws and systems. Oxfam Novib has also supported partners that are working on the provision of humanitarian assistance, response preparedness and conflict transformation. We have also supported partners in the provision of health services, access to quality education and gender justice.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib</narrative>
  </participating-org>
